  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 AM EST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nWe will have one more in our string of pleasant fall days tomorrow, \nwith a chilly start followed by a warm afternoon with high \ntemperatures generally around 4 to 8 degrees above normal for this \ntime of year.\n\nFor Wednesday night a mid/upper level short wave will dig in from \nthe northwest with a surface low moving by to our north, dragging a \ncold front through our area. Rain showers will move across the area \nWednesday night ahead of and near the front. Lapse rates will \nsteepen enough that a few rumbles of thunder cannot be ruled out \nWednesday night, although convective energy is still looking \nextremely limited. \n\nA taste of more winter-like conditions can be expected Thursday \nthrough Saturday as models continue to show an upper low sinking \nsouth across the Great Lakes region before shifting southeast and \nthen east off the northeast US coast. Multiple short waves rotating \naround this upper low will bring additional surges of colder air, \nthe first of which will move in later Thursday and Thursday night. \nThe details are still not as clear as we would like, but we will \nlikely see showers at times enhanced by the short wave energy lobes, \nand these showers will transition to snow showers over the higher \nterrain of E TN and SW VA by Thursday night as the colder air surges \nin. Northwest 850 mb winds of 15 to 25 kts at times during this \nperiod will help to enhance snow showers in the favored upslope snow \nareas of the mountains, and light snow accumulations continue to \nlook likely over the higher elevations Thursday night through Friday \nnight. Some snow flurries may even be seen across lower elevation \nareas of SW VA and the northern half of E TN especially late Friday \ninto Friday night. Precipitation chances will come to an end on \nSaturday as the upper low moves off the coast and the northwest flow \nover our area weakens.  \n\nBuilding high pressure across the TN-OH Valley region will bring dry \nconditions for early next week, with below normal temperatures \ndespite some gradual warming.",
